# `needs no npm` negates the verb through its object, the noun the verb
# acts on, so the reader meets the noun before learning the sentence is
# negative. Moving the negation onto the verb (`does not need npm`) puts
# it first. Linguists call the first form no-negation, after its `no`,
# and the second not-negation. Only `need` and `require` count, because
# `has no header` and `takes no arguments` read as plain idiom. A
# comparison word after `no` (`requires no more than 2 GB`, `need no
# longer wait`) states a bound or a time instead, so the guard skips
# `more`, `less`, `fewer`, and `longer`. The rule flags instead of
# replacing, because the fix depends on tense and person (`does not
# need`, `do not need`, `did not need`).
expr main ([needs, need, needed, requires, require, required] no ![more, less, fewer, longer])
let message "No-negation. Negate the verb, so needs no npm becomes does not need npm."
let description "Flags need and require negated through their object."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Consuming sites do not need npm."
allows "The file has no header."
allows "The command takes no arguments."
allows "The build requires no more than 2 GB."
